"""A Level Pure Mathematics with Mechanics (Lower Sixth): Forces and Newton's laws.

Written for KKEVO Academy. Standard A Level mechanics (g = 9.8 m/s^2 unless stated), not official Board text.
All data in worked examples and practice questions are invented for practice.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("pure-mathematics-with-mechanics", "lower-sixth")

MODULE = "Forces & Newton's Laws"

LESSONS = []

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Forces and equilibrium
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MODULE,
    topic="Forces and equilibrium",
    title="Resultants, resolving and equilibrium of a particle",
    slug="mel2-resolving-forces-equilibrium",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Replace a set of forces by components, find the resultant, and use the two equilibrium equations to find tensions, unknown forces and angles.",
    objectives=[
        "Resolve a force into two perpendicular components and add components to find a resultant",
        "State the conditions for a particle to be in equilibrium",
        "Find tensions and unknown forces for a particle held by two strings or pushed on a surface",
        "Find an unknown force and the angle at which it acts",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Resultant", "Components", "Equilibrium", "Tension", "Normal reaction", "Weight"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A taxi driver in Douala hangs a small mirror from the roof of his car by two cords. A load of timber hangs from two ropes at a sawmill in Mbalmayo. In both cases nothing moves, so the forces on the object **balance**. Mechanics turns that fact into equations and lets you find the tension in each rope before one of them snaps."),
        T(r"You met resolving forces and the smooth slope in Form 5. Here we put the ideas together: we add several forces at different angles to find a **resultant**, and we solve **equilibrium** problems where an angle or a force is unknown. We use $g = 9.8\ \text{m/s}^2$ unless a question says otherwise."),
        D(r"A **particle** is an object whose size is ignored, so all forces act at one point. A **light** string has no mass, so the tension is the same at both ends. A **smooth** surface exerts no friction. A **rough** surface exerts friction. The **normal reaction** $R$ is the push of a surface at right angles to it. **Weight** is $W = mg$, acting vertically downwards. **Tension** pulls along a string; a rod can also give a **thrust** (a push).", title="Words you must know"),
        D(r"A force $F$ at an angle $\theta$ to a chosen direction has a component $F\cos\theta$ **along** that direction and $F\sin\theta$ **perpendicular** to it. Choose two perpendicular directions (horizontal and vertical, or along and across a slope) and resolve every force in the same two directions.", title="Components of a force", formula=r"F\cos\theta \ \ \text{(next to the angle)}, \qquad F\sin\theta \ \ \text{(opposite the angle)}"),
        QUIZ(r"A force of 50 N acts at $40^\circ$ above the horizontal. What is its horizontal component?",
             [r"$32.1$ N", r"$38.3$ N", r"$50$ N", r"$76.6$ N"], 1,
             r"The angle is next to the horizontal, so the horizontal component is $50\cos 40^\circ = 38.3$ N (to 3 s.f.).",
             hint=r"Is the $40^\circ$ next to the horizontal side of the triangle, or opposite it?",
             feedback=[r"$50\sin 40^\circ = 32.1$ N is the **vertical** component.", "", "A component can never be larger than the force itself, and 50 N would mean the force is exactly horizontal.", r"This is $50/\cos 40^\circ$: dividing instead of multiplying. A component is always smaller than the force."]),
        D(r"The **resultant** of a set of forces is the single force that has the same effect as all of them together. Add the components in each direction: $X = \sum F_x$ and $Y = \sum F_y$. Then the resultant has", title="Finding the resultant", formula=r"R = \sqrt{X^2 + Y^2}, \qquad \tan\alpha = \frac{|Y|}{|X|}", note=r"$\alpha$ is the acute angle between the resultant and the $x$-direction. Look at the signs of $X$ and $Y$ to place the resultant in the correct quadrant."),
        EX("Worked example 1: resultant of three forces", intro=r"Three forces act on a particle: 12 N due east, 9 N at $60^\circ$ north of east, and 5 N due south. Find the magnitude of the resultant and its direction.", steps=[
            S("Take east as $x$ and north as $y$. Resolve each force", math=r"12\ \to\ (12,\,0);\quad 9\ \to\ (9\cos 60^\circ,\ 9\sin 60^\circ) = (4.5,\ 7.794);\quad 5\ \to\ (0,\,-5)"),
            S("Add the components", math=r"X = 12 + 4.5 + 0 = 16.5, \qquad Y = 0 + 7.794 - 5 = 2.794"),
            S("Magnitude of the resultant", math=r"R = \sqrt{16.5^2 + 2.794^2} = 16.7\ \text{N}\ \ (3\text{ s.f.})"),
            S("Direction", math=r"\tan\alpha = \frac{2.794}{16.5} \Rightarrow \alpha = 9.6^\circ", text=r"Both components are positive, so the resultant points $9.6^\circ$ north of east."),
        ], check=r"16.5^2 + 2.794^2 = 280.1 = 16.73^2"),
        H("Equilibrium of a particle"),
        D(r"A particle is in **equilibrium** when the resultant force on it is zero. Then it is at rest or moves in a straight line at constant speed. In components, the forces must balance in **both** perpendicular directions.", title="Equilibrium", formula=r"\sum F_x = 0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad \sum F_y = 0"),
        T(r"So an equilibrium problem gives you **two** equations. That lets you find **two** unknowns: two tensions, or one force and one angle. For three forces you can also draw a closed triangle of forces and use the sine rule (Lami's theorem); check which method your textbook prefers. Resolving always works."),
        IMG("apomel2-two-strings.svg", alt="A lamp of mass 4 kg hangs from a ceiling by two strings that make 30 degrees and 50 degrees with the horizontal, with the weight 39.2 newtons acting downwards and tensions 25.6 and 34.5 newtons",
            caption="Figure 1. A lamp of mass 4 kg held by two light strings (Worked example 2)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: two strings", intro=r"A lamp of mass 4 kg hangs in equilibrium from two light strings. The strings make $30^\circ$ and $50^\circ$ with the horizontal ceiling (Figure 1). Find the tension in each string.", steps=[
            S("Weight of the lamp", math=r"W = 4 \times 9.8 = 39.2\ \text{N}"),
            S("Resolve horizontally (the tensions are the only horizontal forces)", math=r"T_1\cos 30^\circ = T_2\cos 50^\circ \quad\Rightarrow\quad T_1 = \frac{\cos 50^\circ}{\cos 30^\circ}\,T_2 = 0.7422\,T_2"),
            S("Resolve vertically", math=r"T_1\sin 30^\circ + T_2\sin 50^\circ = 39.2"),
            S("Substitute $T_1$ and solve for $T_2$", math=r"0.7422(0.5)\,T_2 + 0.7660\,T_2 = 39.2 \;\Rightarrow\; 1.1371\,T_2 = 39.2 \;\Rightarrow\; T_2 = 34.5\ \text{N}"),
            S("Find $T_1$", math=r"T_1 = 0.7422 \times 34.47 = 25.6\ \text{N}", text="The steeper string ($50^\\circ$) carries the bigger tension because it supports more of the weight."),
        ], check=r"25.59\cos 30^\circ = 22.16 = 34.47\cos 50^\circ"),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong trig function for the angle", r"If a string makes $30^\circ$ with the **horizontal**, its horizontal component is $T\cos 30^\circ$. If the angle is given from the **vertical**, sine and cosine swap. Always say which line the angle is measured from."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a particle pulled sideways", intro=r"A particle of weight 40 N hangs from a string. A horizontal force $Q$ pulls it sideways until the string makes $35^\circ$ with the vertical and the particle is in equilibrium. Find the tension $T$ in the string and the force $Q$.", steps=[
            S("Resolve vertically", math=r"T\cos 35^\circ = 40 \;\Rightarrow\; T = \frac{40}{\cos 35^\circ} = 48.8\ \text{N}"),
            S("Resolve horizontally", math=r"Q = T\sin 35^\circ = 40\tan 35^\circ = 28.0\ \text{N}"),
        ], check=r"48.83^2 = 40^2 + 28.01^2"),
        H("Unknown force and unknown angle"),
        EX("Worked example 4: finding a force and its direction", intro=r"A particle is in equilibrium under three horizontal forces: 20 N due east, 15 N due north and a third force $P$. Find the magnitude of $P$ and the direction in which it acts.", steps=[
            S("Resolve east and north. Let $P$ have components $P_x$ (east) and $P_y$ (north)", math=r"20 + P_x = 0, \qquad 15 + P_y = 0"),
            S("So $P_x = -20$ and $P_y = -15$", text="$P$ points west and south."),
            S("Magnitude", math=r"P = \sqrt{20^2 + 15^2} = 25\ \text{N}"),
            S("Direction", math=r"\tan\theta = \frac{15}{20} \Rightarrow \theta = 36.9^\circ", text="$P$ acts $36.9^\\circ$ below the westward direction (south of west)."),
        ], check=r"25\cos 36.87^\circ = 20,\ \ 25\sin 36.87^\circ = 15"),
        T(r"Notice what happened: the unknown force is exactly **equal and opposite** to the resultant of the known forces. That is true in every equilibrium problem and gives a quick check."),
        H("A particle on a horizontal surface"),
        T(r"A force that is not horizontal changes the normal reaction. A crate of mass 20 kg is pushed along a floor by a force of 60 N directed $30^\circ$ **below** the horizontal. Vertically, the floor must support the weight **and** the downward part of the push, so $R = mg + P\sin 30^\circ = 196 + 30 = 226$ N. If the same force pulled the crate **upwards** at $30^\circ$, then $R = 196 - 30 = 166$ N. This idea is the key to friction problems later in this module."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting a force", r"Draw a large diagram first and mark **every** force: weight, tension(s), normal reaction, applied forces. Then resolve. Most wrong answers come from a missing force."),
        MISTAKE("Writing $R = mg$ when a force is at an angle", r"The normal reaction equals the weight only when there are no other vertical forces. Resolve vertically every time."),
        QUIZ(r"A particle is in equilibrium under three forces. Two are 6 N east and 8 N north. What is the magnitude of the third force?",
             [r"$2$ N", r"$14$ N", r"$10$ N", r"$48$ N"], 2,
             r"The third force is equal and opposite to the resultant of the other two: $\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = 10$ N.",
             hint="What must the third force do to the resultant of the other two?",
             feedback=[r"You subtracted $8 - 6$. The forces are at right angles, so use Pythagoras.", r"You added $6 + 8$. Forces at right angles do not add like that.", "", r"You multiplied $6 \times 8$."]),
        PQ(r"A sign of mass 12 kg hangs from two light strings of equal length attached to a horizontal beam. Each string makes $40^\circ$ with the beam. Find the tension in each string.",
           r"The strings are symmetrical so the tensions are equal, $T$. Vertically: $2T\sin 40^\circ = 12 \times 9.8 = 117.6$, so $T = \dfrac{117.6}{2\sin 40^\circ} = 91.5$ N (3 s.f.)."),
        PQ(r"Forces of 8 N, 10 N and 6 N act on a particle at $0^\circ$, $90^\circ$ and $225^\circ$ measured anticlockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the magnitude of the resultant.",
           r"$X = 8 + 6\cos 225^\circ = 8 - 4.243 = 3.757$ and $Y = 10 + 6\sin 225^\circ = 10 - 4.243 = 5.757$. $R = \sqrt{3.757^2 + 5.757^2} = 6.87$ N (3 s.f.)."),
        PQ(r"A particle of mass 3 kg rests in equilibrium on a horizontal floor. It is held by a light string pulled at $25^\circ$ above the horizontal with tension 10 N, and a horizontal force $H$ in the opposite direction. Find $H$ and the normal reaction $R$.",
           r"Horizontally: $H = 10\cos 25^\circ = 9.06$ N. Vertically: $R + 10\sin 25^\circ = 3 \times 9.8 = 29.4$, so $R = 29.4 - 4.226 = 25.2$ N."),
        PQ(r"A particle of weight 30 N is in equilibrium under a force $P$ at $20^\circ$ above the horizontal (pointing east) and a force $Q$ at angle $\theta$ above the horizontal (pointing west). Given that $P = 50$ N, find $Q$ and $\theta$.",
           r"Horizontally: $Q\cos\theta = 50\cos 20^\circ = 46.98$. Vertically: $Q\sin\theta + 50\sin 20^\circ = 30$, so $Q\sin\theta = 12.90$. Then $Q = \sqrt{46.98^2 + 12.90^2} = 48.7$ N and $\tan\theta = \dfrac{12.90}{46.98}$, so $\theta = 15.4^\circ$."),
        TIP(r"In the exam: forces and equilibrium can appear in the mechanics section of Paper 1 (multiple choice) and in the option paper (Paper 3, essay questions). Always draw a force diagram and state the two equations you are resolving with before you calculate."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Resolve a force $F$ at angle $\theta$: $F\cos\theta$ next to the angle, $F\sin\theta$ opposite it.",
            r"Resultant: add components, then $R = \sqrt{X^2 + Y^2}$ and $\tan\alpha = |Y|/|X|$.",
            r"Equilibrium means $\sum F_x = 0$ and $\sum F_y = 0$: two equations, two unknowns.",
            r"With a force at an angle, the normal reaction is **not** $mg$: resolve perpendicular to the surface.",
            r"The balancing force is equal and opposite to the resultant of the others."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. Newton's second law and connected particles in a line
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MODULE,
    topic="Newton's laws and connected particles",
    title="Newton's second law and particles connected in a line",
    slug="mel2-newton-second-law-connected",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Apply F = ma to a single particle (lifts, ropes, angled pulls) and to two particles joined by a string or tow-bar, using the whole system and then one particle to find the tension.",
    objectives=[
        "State Newton's three laws and use the second law $F = ma$ with the resultant force",
        "Solve problems on lifts, vertical ropes and angled pulls on smooth and level surfaces",
        "Find the acceleration and the tension for two particles joined by a light string or tow-bar",
        "Combine $F = ma$ with the constant-acceleration equations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Newton's second law", "Resultant force", "Tension", "Thrust", "System of particles", "Apparent weight"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A lorry leaves Douala for Yaoundé pulling a loaded trailer. When the driver presses the accelerator, how quickly does the whole load speed up, and how hard does the tow-bar pull on the trailer? A lift in a hospital in Bamenda starts to rise, and you feel heavier for a moment. Both questions are answered by Newton's laws."),
        H("Newton's laws"),
        D(r"**First law:** a particle stays at rest or moves with constant velocity unless a resultant force acts on it. **Second law:** the resultant force on a particle equals its mass times its acceleration, in the direction of the resultant force. **Third law:** if body A exerts a force on body B, then B exerts an equal and opposite force on A.", title="Newton's laws of motion", formula=r"F_{\text{resultant}} = ma"),
        T(r"The unit of force is the **newton** (N): one newton gives a mass of 1 kg an acceleration of $1\ \text{m/s}^2$. Notice that the second law uses the **resultant** force, not any single force. The first law is the special case $a = 0$, which is equilibrium from the last lesson."),
        KEY("A routine that always works", "", cards=[
            ["Draw", "A large diagram. Mark every force on each particle and the direction of acceleration."],
            ["Choose", "A positive direction, the direction in which the particle accelerates."],
            ["Write", r"(forces in the positive direction) $-$ (forces against) $= ma$."],
            ["Solve", "Then check the units and whether the answer is sensible."],
        ]),
        H("A single particle: lifts and ropes"),
        T(r"Take a person standing in a lift. Two forces act on them: the weight $mg$ downwards and the **normal reaction** $R$ of the floor upwards. The reading of a spring balance would be $R$. It is bigger than $mg$ when the lift accelerates **upwards** and smaller when it accelerates **downwards**."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a lift", intro=r"A woman of mass 60 kg stands on the floor of a lift. Find the reaction of the floor on her when the lift (a) accelerates upwards at $1.2\ \text{m/s}^2$, (b) has an upward velocity and is slowing down at $1.5\ \text{m/s}^2$.", steps=[
            S("(a) Take upwards as positive. The acceleration is $+1.2$", math=r"R - 60(9.8) = 60(1.2)"),
            S("Solve", math=r"R = 588 + 72 = 660\ \text{N}"),
            S("(b) Slowing down while moving up means the acceleration is **downwards**, $-1.5$", math=r"R - 588 = 60(-1.5)"),
            S("Solve", math=r"R = 588 - 90 = 498\ \text{N}", text="She feels lighter in (b), heavier in (a)."),
        ], check=r"660 - 588 = 72 = 60(1.2)"),
        QUIZ(r"A boy of mass 50 kg stands in a lift that accelerates **downwards** at $2\ \text{m/s}^2$. What is the reaction of the floor on him? (Use $g = 9.8$.)",
             [r"$590$ N", r"$490$ N", r"$100$ N", r"$390$ N"], 3,
             r"Downwards is positive for this motion: $50(9.8) - R = 50(2)$, so $R = 490 - 100 = 390$ N.",
             hint="Which is larger when the lift accelerates down: the weight or the reaction?",
             feedback=[r"This is $490 + 100$. The reaction is smaller than the weight when the acceleration is downwards.", r"This is the reaction when the lift is not accelerating (equal to the weight).", r"This is only $ma$, the resultant force, not the reaction.", ""]),
        H("A force at an angle"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a crate pulled at an angle", intro=r"A crate of mass 5 kg rests on a smooth horizontal floor. A rope pulls it with a force of 25 N at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal. Find (a) the acceleration, (b) the normal reaction, (c) the speed after 3 s from rest, and the distance travelled in that time.", steps=[
            S("(a) Horizontally, only the horizontal part of the pull acts (smooth floor)", math=r"25\cos 30^\circ = 5a \;\Rightarrow\; a = \frac{21.65}{5} = 4.33\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("(b) Vertically there is no acceleration", math=r"R + 25\sin 30^\circ = 5(9.8) \;\Rightarrow\; R = 49 - 12.5 = 36.5\ \text{N}"),
            S(r"(c) Use $v = u + at$ with $u = 0$", math=r"v = 0 + 4.33 \times 3 = 13.0\ \text{m/s}"),
            S(r"Use $s = ut + \tfrac12 at^2$", math=r"s = \tfrac12(4.33)(3)^2 = 19.5\ \text{m}"),
        ], check=r"13.0^2 = 2(4.33)(19.5) = 169"),
        TIP(r"Forces give $a$; $a$ then goes into $v = u + at$, $s = ut + \frac12at^2$, $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$. If the question asks for a speed or a distance, mechanics is a two-stage process."),
        H("Two particles joined in a line"),
        T(r"When a lorry pulls a trailer, the two move together with the **same acceleration** $a$ (the tow-bar is rigid, or the string stays taut). The tow-bar pulls the trailer forward with a force $T$ and, by Newton's third law, the trailer pulls the lorry back with an equal force $T$. A **light** tow-bar or string has the same tension at both ends."),
        IMG("apomel2-lorry-trailer.svg", alt="A lorry of mass 3000 kg pulls a trailer of mass 1200 kg joined by a tow-bar with tension T; the driving force is 9000 N and the resistances are 800 N on the lorry and 400 N on the trailer",
            caption="Figure 1. A lorry and a trailer (Worked example 3)."),
        EX("Worked example 3: lorry and trailer", intro=r"A lorry of mass 3000 kg pulls a trailer of mass 1200 kg along a straight horizontal road (Figure 1). The engine gives a driving force of 9000 N. The resistances to motion are 800 N on the lorry and 400 N on the trailer. Find the acceleration and the tension in the tow-bar.", steps=[
            S("**Whole system** (the tension is internal, so it does not appear). Total mass 4200 kg, total resistance 1200 N", math=r"9000 - 800 - 400 = 4200\,a \;\Rightarrow\; a = \frac{7800}{4200} = 1.857\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("**Trailer alone** (the tension is now an external force)", math=r"T - 400 = 1200a = 2228.6 \;\Rightarrow\; T = 2629\ \text{N}"),
            S("Answers", text=r"$a = 1.86\ \text{m/s}^2$ and $T = 2630$ N, both to 3 s.f."),
        ], check=r"\text{Lorry: } 9000 - 800 - 2628.6 = 5571.4 = 3000(1.857)"),
        KEY("The two-step plan for connected particles", "", cards=[
            ["Step 1", "Treat both particles as one system to find $a$. The internal tension cancels."],
            ["Step 2", "Apply $F = ma$ to the simpler particle alone to find $T$."],
            ["Check", "Use the other particle's equation. It must give the same $T$."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Putting the tension in the whole-system equation", r"For the whole system the tow-bar force is **internal**: it pulls one particle forward and the other back, so it cancels. If you include $T$ you will get a wrong acceleration."),
        MISTAKE("Using $F = ma$ with a force that is not the resultant", r"If the driving force is 9000 N, the acceleration is **not** $9000/3000$. Subtract the resistances first, then divide by the **total** mass that is being accelerated."),
        QUIZ(r"A block of mass 4 kg is pulled along a horizontal floor by a horizontal force of 20 N against a resistance of 8 N. What is its acceleration?",
             [r"$5\ \text{m/s}^2$", r"$7\ \text{m/s}^2$", r"$3\ \text{m/s}^2$", r"$12\ \text{m/s}^2$"], 2,
             r"The resultant force is $20 - 8 = 12$ N, so $a = 12/4 = 3\ \text{m/s}^2$.",
             hint="Find the resultant force before dividing by the mass.",
             feedback=[r"You divided the 20 N by 4 and ignored the resistance.", r"You added the force and the resistance: $28/4$.", "", r"This is the resultant force 12 N, not the acceleration. You forgot to divide by the mass."]),
        MISTAKE("Wrong sign for the acceleration", r"Decide the positive direction first. A lift that is **moving up and slowing** has a downward acceleration."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting units", r"Forces in N, masses in kg, accelerations in $\text{m/s}^2$. Write the unit on every final answer."),
        PQ(r"A car of mass 1200 kg is driven along a straight level road with a driving force of 3000 N against a total resistance of 600 N. Find its acceleration and the time to reach $20\ \text{m/s}$ from rest.",
           r"$3000 - 600 = 1200a$, so $a = 2\ \text{m/s}^2$. From $v = u + at$: $20 = 2t$, so $t = 10$ s."),
        PQ(r"A lift of total mass 660 kg (cage and passengers) is raised by a cable with an upward acceleration of $0.8\ \text{m/s}^2$. Find the tension in the cable. A passenger of mass 70 kg is in the lift: find the force of the floor on her.",
           r"Cable: $T - 660(9.8) = 660(0.8)$, so $T = 6468 + 528 = 6996$ N, about $7000$ N. Passenger: $R - 70(9.8) = 70(0.8)$, so $R = 686 + 56 = 742$ N."),
        PQ(r"A tractor of mass 2500 kg pulls a trailer of mass 1500 kg along a horizontal road. The driving force is 6000 N and the resistances are 500 N on the tractor and 300 N on the trailer. Find the acceleration and the tension in the tow-bar.",
           r"System: $6000 - 800 = 4000a$, so $a = 1.3\ \text{m/s}^2$. Trailer: $T - 300 = 1500(1.3)$, so $T = 2250$ N. Check with the tractor: $6000 - 500 - 2250 = 3250 = 2500(1.3)$."),
        PQ(r"A block of mass 8 kg is pulled along a smooth horizontal floor by a light string inclined at $40^\circ$ above the horizontal. The tension is 20 N. Find the acceleration of the block and the normal reaction.",
           r"Horizontally: $20\cos 40^\circ = 8a$, so $a = 1.92\ \text{m/s}^2$. Vertically: $R + 20\sin 40^\circ = 8(9.8) = 78.4$, so $R = 78.4 - 12.86 = 65.5$ N."),
        TIP(r"In the exam: Newton's second law with connected particles is a named topic in the Board's report on the option paper (Paper 3, mechanics), and it can also appear in the multiple-choice mechanics section of Paper 1. Always state which particle or system you apply $F = ma$ to."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Newton's second law: resultant force $= ma$, in the direction of the resultant.",
            r"Choose a positive direction (the way the particle accelerates) and write: forces for $-$ forces against $= ma$.",
            r"In a lift, $R = m(g + a)$ going up faster, and $R = m(g - a)$ accelerating downwards.",
            r"Particles joined by a taut light string or rigid bar have the same acceleration; the tension is equal at both ends.",
            r"Use the whole system to find $a$ (tension cancels), then one particle to find $T$, and check with the other."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. Pulleys and particles on tables
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MODULE,
    topic="Newton's laws and connected particles",
    title="Particles over pulleys and on tables",
    slug="mel2-pulleys-and-tables",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Solve two-particle problems where a string passes over a smooth pulley: two hanging masses, or one mass on a smooth table, find the force on the pulley, and handle the string going slack.",
    objectives=[
        "Write a separate equation of motion for each particle and solve for the acceleration and the tension",
        "Handle a particle on a smooth table joined over a pulley to a hanging particle",
        "Find the force exerted by the string on the pulley, using the angle between the two parts of the string",
        "Analyse the motion after a particle hits the ground and the string goes slack",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Smooth pulley", "Light inextensible string", "Tension", "Force on the pulley", "Slack string"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A woman at a village well lets a bucket go down while a stone counterweight goes up. A trader drags a sack along a table by letting a smaller sack fall over the edge. Both situations involve a string passing over a **pulley**. This lesson builds on Newton's second law for connected particles."),
        H("Assumptions and the method"),
        D(r"Unless told otherwise we assume that the string is **light** (no mass) and **inextensible** (cannot stretch), and that the pulley is **smooth** and **light**. Then (1) the tension is the **same** on both sides of the pulley, and (2) the two particles have the **same acceleration magnitude**, because the string cannot stretch.", title="The standard model"),
        KEY("Method for pulley problems", "", cards=[
            ["Draw", "Mark the tension $T$ (the same everywhere in the string), the weights, and the direction of motion of each particle."],
            ["Write", "$F = ma$ for **each** particle separately, with its own positive direction (the way it moves)."],
            ["Solve", "Add the two equations to remove $T$, find $a$, then substitute back to find $T$."],
        ]),
        H("Two particles hanging over a pulley"),
        IMG("apomel2-atwood.svg", alt="Two particles of mass 5 kg and 3 kg hang from the ends of a light string over a smooth fixed pulley; the 5 kg particle moves down and the 3 kg particle up with the same acceleration a",
            caption="Figure 1. Two hanging particles, 5 kg and 3 kg (Worked example 1)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: two hanging masses", intro=r"Particles of mass 5 kg and 3 kg are joined by a light inextensible string that passes over a smooth fixed pulley (Figure 1). The system is released from rest with both parts of the string vertical and taut. Find the acceleration and the tension in the string, and the force exerted on the pulley.", steps=[
            S("The 5 kg particle goes down. Take down as positive for it", math=r"5(9.8) - T = 5a"),
            S("The 3 kg particle goes up. Take up as positive for it", math=r"T - 3(9.8) = 3a"),
            S("Add the equations (the $T$ cancels)", math=r"2(9.8) = 8a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 2.45\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("Substitute into the second equation", math=r"T = 3(9.8 + 2.45) = 36.75\ \text{N}"),
            S("Force on the pulley: the string pulls down with $T$ on **both** sides", math=r"2T = 73.5\ \text{N}\ \text{(vertically downwards)}"),
        ], check=r"5(9.8) - 36.75 = 12.25 = 5(2.45)"),
        T(r"For general masses $m_1 > m_2$ you can quote the results $a = \dfrac{(m_1 - m_2)g}{m_1 + m_2}$ and $T = \dfrac{2m_1m_2g}{m_1 + m_2}$, but you must still be able to **derive** them in an exam: show the two equations."),
        H("A particle on a smooth table"),
        IMG("apomel2-pulley-table.svg", alt="Particle A of mass 4 kg on a smooth horizontal table joined by a string over a smooth pulley at the table edge to particle B of mass 2 kg hanging; both have acceleration a",
            caption="Figure 2. A on a smooth table, B hanging (Worked example 2)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: table and pulley", intro=r"Particle A of mass 4 kg lies on a smooth horizontal table. A light string joins it to particle B of mass 2 kg, hanging freely, and passes over a smooth pulley at the edge of the table (Figure 2). The system is released from rest. Find (a) the acceleration, (b) the tension, (c) the magnitude and direction of the force the string exerts on the pulley.", steps=[
            S("A moves horizontally. The only horizontal force on A is the tension", math=r"T = 4a"),
            S("B moves down", math=r"2(9.8) - T = 2a"),
            S("Add", math=r"19.6 = 6a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 3.27\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("Tension", math=r"T = 4a = 13.1\ \text{N}"),
            S("(c) One part of the string is horizontal and the other vertical, so the two tensions are at $90^\\circ$. The force on the pulley is their resultant", math=r"\sqrt{T^2 + T^2} = T\sqrt{2} = 13.07\sqrt{2} = 18.5\ \text{N}", text=r"It acts at $45^\circ$ below the horizontal, pointing down and towards the table."),
        ], check=r"19.6 - 13.07 = 6.53 = 2(3.267)"),
        MISTAKE("Saying $T$ equals the weight of the hanging particle", r"The tension equals the weight only if the particle is **not accelerating**. Here B accelerates downwards, so $T < 2g$."),
        H("The force on the pulley"),
        T(r"The string pulls on the pulley with a force $T$ along **each** part of the string, directed away from the pulley towards the particles. The pulley's force is the **resultant of these two forces**. The Board's report on this topic says many candidates did not use the angle at the pulley and instead used $F = ma$ with the masses and accelerations of the particles. The angle between the string parts is what you need."),
        D(r"If the two parts of the string leave the pulley in directions that make an angle $\varphi$ with each other, the resultant of the two tensions has magnitude $2T\cos\dfrac{\varphi}{2}$ and acts along the **bisector** of the angle between the two parts.", title="Force on a smooth pulley", formula=r"F_{\text{pulley}} = 2T\cos\frac{\varphi}{2}", note=r"Both parts vertical: $\varphi = 0^\circ$, so $2T$. One horizontal and one vertical: $\varphi = 90^\circ$, so $2T\cos 45^\circ = T\sqrt{2}$."),
        QUIZ(r"The tension in a light string over a smooth pulley is 20 N. The two parts of the string are perpendicular. What is the magnitude of the force of the string on the pulley?",
             [r"$40$ N", r"$20$ N", r"$14.1$ N", r"$28.3$ N"], 3,
             r"The resultant of two 20 N forces at $90^\circ$ is $20\sqrt{2} = 28.3$ N.",
             hint=r"Use $2T\cos(\varphi/2)$ with the angle between the two parts of the string.",
             feedback=[r"$40$ N is $2T$, correct only when both parts are vertical (the angle between them is $0^\circ$).", r"$20$ N is a single tension, but the string pulls the pulley in two directions.", r"$14.1$ N is $T/\sqrt{2}$: you used $T\sin 45^\circ$ for just one component.", ""]),
        H("When the string goes slack"),
        T(r"When a hanging particle hits the floor, it stops, and the string becomes **slack**: the tension is zero. The other particle is then moving freely. On a smooth table it moves on at **constant velocity**. If it is a hanging particle, it moves under gravity alone, with acceleration $g$ downwards, so it continues upwards before stopping."),
        EX("Worked example 3: the string goes slack", intro=r"In Worked example 1 (5 kg and 3 kg particles) the 5 kg particle is released from rest at a height of 1.2 m above the floor. Find the greatest height above its starting position reached by the 3 kg particle.", steps=[
            S("Stage 1: the 5 kg particle falls 1.2 m with $a = 2.45\\ \\text{m/s}^2$ from rest", math=r"v^2 = 0 + 2(2.45)(1.2) = 5.88"),
            S("Stage 2: the 5 kg particle stops and the string goes slack. The 3 kg particle has $v^2 = 5.88$ upwards and now decelerates at $g$", math=r"0 = 5.88 - 2(9.8)h \;\Rightarrow\; h = 0.3\ \text{m}"),
            S("Total rise of the 3 kg particle", math=r"1.2 + 0.3 = 1.5\ \text{m}"),
        ], check=r"v = \sqrt{5.88} = 2.42\ \text{m/s at the moment the string goes slack}"),
        TIP(r"Always split a motion into stages: while the string is taut (use $a$ from the pulley equations), and after it goes slack (use $a = g$ or $a = 0$). The speed at the end of one stage is the starting speed of the next."),
        MISTAKE("Using one equation for the whole system with $T$ in it", r"Write a separate equation for each particle, or use one equation for the whole system **without** $T$. Never mix the two forms."),
        MISTAKE("A different positive direction for the same particle", r"Choose a positive direction for each particle that is its direction of motion, and keep it for the whole question."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong force on the pulley", r"The force on the pulley is the resultant of two tensions. It is not $T$, not $2T$ in every case, and not found from $ma$."),
        PQ(r"Particles of mass 4 kg and 6 kg are attached to the ends of a light string that passes over a smooth fixed pulley. The system is released from rest with the string taut. Find the acceleration and the tension.",
           r"Heavier (6 kg) goes down: $6(9.8) - T = 6a$. Lighter goes up: $T - 4(9.8) = 4a$. Add: $19.6 = 10a$, so $a = 1.96\ \text{m/s}^2$. $T = 4(9.8 + 1.96) = 47.0$ N."),
        PQ(r"Particle A of mass 3 kg lies on a smooth horizontal table and is joined by a light string over a smooth pulley at the edge of the table to particle B of mass 5 kg, which hangs. B starts from rest 0.8 m above the floor. Find (a) the acceleration and the tension while B falls, (b) the speed of B as it hits the floor, (c) the time A takes to move a further 2 m afterwards. Assume A does not reach the pulley.",
           r"(a) $T = 3a$ and $5(9.8) - T = 5a$, so $49 = 8a$, $a = 6.125\ \text{m/s}^2$, $T = 18.375$ N. (b) $v^2 = 2(6.125)(0.8) = 9.8$, so $v = 3.13\ \text{m/s}$. (c) After B lands the string is slack and A moves at constant speed $3.13\ \text{m/s}$, so $t = 2/3.13 = 0.639$ s."),
        PQ(r"Particles of mass 2 kg and 0.5 kg hang on either side of a smooth fixed pulley, joined by a light string. The 2 kg particle is released from rest 1 m above the floor. Find the further distance the 0.5 kg particle rises after the 2 kg particle hits the floor.",
           r"While taut: $a = \dfrac{(2 - 0.5)(9.8)}{2.5} = 5.88\ \text{m/s}^2$. $v^2 = 2(5.88)(1) = 11.76$. After the string goes slack: $0 = 11.76 - 2(9.8)h$, so $h = 0.6$ m."),
        PQ(r"A light string passes over a smooth pulley. The tension in the string is 20 N and the two parts of the string make an angle of $60^\circ$ with each other. Find the magnitude of the force of the string on the pulley, and state its direction.",
           r"$2T\cos 30^\circ = 2(20)(0.866) = 34.6$ N. It acts along the bisector of the angle between the two parts of the string, pointing away from the pulley between the particles."),
        TIP(r"In the exam: connected particles over a pulley are a named topic in the Board's report on the option paper (Paper 3, mechanics). The report notes that candidates lost marks on the force exerted on the pulley by not using the angle between the string parts. Say 'the tension is the same on both sides because the pulley is smooth and the string light' in your answer."),
        SUM(r"For a light string over a smooth pulley, the tension is the same in both parts and the accelerations have the same magnitude.",
            r"Write $F = ma$ for each particle, add to remove $T$, find $a$, then find $T$.",
            r"On a smooth table: $T = m_Aa$ for the table particle, and $m_Bg - T = m_Ba$ for the hanging particle.",
            r"Force on the pulley $= 2T\cos(\varphi/2)$, where $\varphi$ is the angle between the two parts of the string.",
            r"When the hanging particle lands, $T = 0$: a particle on a smooth table moves at constant speed; a hanging one decelerates at $g$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. Friction
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MODULE,
    topic="Friction",
    title="The friction law and the coefficient of friction",
    slug="mel2-friction-law",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use F is at most mu R: decide whether a particle on a rough horizontal surface stays at rest, find the friction force, the coefficient of friction and the acceleration when it slides.",
    objectives=[
        "State the friction law and the meaning of limiting friction",
        "Decide whether a particle on a rough horizontal surface is at rest or sliding",
        "Find the normal reaction, the friction force and the acceleration when the applied force is horizontal or at an angle",
        "Find the coefficient of friction from equilibrium or motion data, and solve connected-particle problems with a rough table",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Friction", "Normal reaction", "Limiting friction", "Coefficient of friction", "Rough surface"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A trader at the Marché Mokolo in Yaoundé pushes a loaded handcart. A gentle push does nothing: the cart stays still. A harder push and it suddenly starts to roll. What is holding the cart back, and why does it give way? The answer is **friction**, a force between two surfaces in contact that opposes their sliding past each other."),
        H("The friction law"),
        D(r"Friction acts **along** the contact surface, in the direction that opposes motion (or the **tendency** to move). Its size is whatever is needed to prevent sliding, up to a maximum value that depends on the normal reaction $R$ and on the surfaces.", title="The friction law", formula=r"F \le \mu R", note=r"$\mu$ is the **coefficient of friction**. It has no unit. A smooth surface has $\mu = 0$."),
        T(r"When the particle is about to slip, friction has reached its maximum value $\mu R$. This situation is called **limiting equilibrium**, and $F = \mu R$ is the **limiting friction**. When the particle is sliding, we model the friction as $F = \mu R$ as well, acting **opposite to the direction of motion**."),
        IMG("apomel2-friction-block.svg", alt="A block on a rough horizontal surface with weight W downwards, normal reaction R upwards, an applied force P to the right and friction F to the left", caption="Figure 1. The four forces on a block pushed along a rough floor."),
        IMG("apomel2-friction-graph.svg", alt="Graph of friction F against applied force P: a straight line F equals P up to the value mu R, then a horizontal line at mu R", caption="Figure 2. Friction matches the push until it reaches $\\mu R$. After that the block slides and friction stays at $\\mu R$."),
        KEY("Three situations", "", cards=[
            [r"At rest, not limiting", r"Friction is found from **equilibrium**, $F = P$ here. It is **less than** $\mu R$. Do **not** use $\mu R$."],
            [r"About to slip (limiting)", r"$F = \mu R$ and $a = 0$. Use this to find $\mu$ or the smallest force that moves the particle."],
            [r"Sliding", r"$F = \mu R$, opposing the motion, and $F_{\text{resultant}} = ma$."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Always writing $F = \\mu R$", r"The formula $F = \mu R$ holds only when the particle is **sliding or about to slide**. For a particle at rest and not about to move, friction is just the force that balances the others, and $F < \mu R$."),
        H("Is the particle at rest?"),
        EX("Worked example 1: pushing a crate", intro=r"A crate of mass 25 kg rests on a rough horizontal floor with coefficient of friction $0.4$. A horizontal force of 80 N is applied to it. Does the crate move? What is the friction force?", steps=[
            S("Normal reaction (no vertical acceleration)", math=r"R = 25(9.8) = 245\ \text{N}"),
            S("Maximum friction available", math=r"F_{\max} = \mu R = 0.4 \times 245 = 98\ \text{N}"),
            S("Compare with the push", text=r"The push (80 N) is **less** than 98 N, so friction can balance it."),
            S("Conclusion", text=r"The crate does **not** move. The friction force is $F = 80$ N, not 98 N."),
        ], check=r"80 \le 98"),
        QUIZ(r"A box of weight 100 N rests on a rough horizontal floor with $\mu = 0.3$. A horizontal force of 20 N acts on it. What is the friction force?",
             [r"$30$ N", r"$20$ N", r"$10$ N", r"$0$ N"], 1,
             r"The maximum friction is $0.3 \times 100 = 30$ N. The push (20 N) is less than 30 N, so the box stays at rest and friction is exactly 20 N, balancing the push.",
             hint="Compare the push with the maximum friction first.",
             feedback=[r"$30$ N is the **maximum** friction $\mu R$. It is only reached when the box is about to move.", "", r"$10$ N is $30 - 20$: friction is not the difference between the limit and the push.", r"The push is trying to move the box, so friction must oppose it: zero friction is impossible here."]),
        H("A force at an angle changes the friction"),
        T(r"The friction depends on $R$, and $R$ changes when an applied force has a vertical part. **Pulling upwards** at an angle reduces $R$ and so reduces friction. **Pushing downwards** increases $R$ and so increases friction."),
        EX("Worked example 2: pulling a sack with a rope", intro=r"A sack of mass 30 kg is pulled along a rough horizontal floor by a rope inclined at $25^\circ$ above the horizontal, with tension 120 N. The coefficient of friction is $0.3$. Find the acceleration.", steps=[
            S("Resolve vertically", math=r"R + 120\sin 25^\circ = 30(9.8) \;\Rightarrow\; R = 294 - 50.7 = 243.3\ \text{N}"),
            S("Friction while sliding", math=r"F = \mu R = 0.3 \times 243.3 = 73.0\ \text{N}"),
            S("Resolve horizontally, $F = ma$", math=r"120\cos 25^\circ - 73.0 = 30a"),
            S("Solve", math=r"108.76 - 73.0 = 30a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 1.19\ \text{m/s}^2"),
        ], check=r"108.76 > 73.0, \text{ so the sack does move}"),
        TIP(r"Before you use $F = ma$ for a sliding particle, check that it really moves: the horizontal pull must be greater than the limiting friction $\mu R$."),
        H("Finding the coefficient of friction"),
        EX("Worked example 3: finding $\\mu$ from motion", intro=r"A block of mass 10 kg is pulled from rest along a rough horizontal surface by a horizontal force of 50 N. After 4 s it has a speed of $6\ \text{m/s}$. Find the coefficient of friction.", steps=[
            S("Acceleration from $v = u + at$", math=r"6 = 0 + 4a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 1.5\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("Newton's second law horizontally", math=r"50 - F = 10(1.5) \;\Rightarrow\; F = 35\ \text{N}"),
            S("Normal reaction", math=r"R = 10(9.8) = 98\ \text{N}"),
            S("Sliding, so $F = \\mu R$", math=r"\mu = \frac{35}{98} = 0.357"),
        ], check=r"0.357 \times 98 = 35"),
        H("A rough table with a hanging particle"),
        EX("Worked example 4: rough table and pulley", intro=r"Particle A of mass 3 kg lies on a rough horizontal table with coefficient of friction $0.5$. It is joined by a light string over a smooth pulley at the edge of the table to particle B of mass 2 kg, hanging freely. The system is released from rest. Show that it moves, and find the acceleration and the tension.", steps=[
            S("Will it move? The pull on A is the weight of B at most", math=r"2(9.8) = 19.6\ \text{N} \quad\text{versus}\quad F_{\max} = 0.5 \times 3(9.8) = 14.7\ \text{N}"),
            S("Since $19.6 > 14.7$, the system moves. For A (towards the pulley)", math=r"T - 14.7 = 3a"),
            S("For B (downwards)", math=r"2(9.8) - T = 2a"),
            S("Add and solve", math=r"19.6 - 14.7 = 5a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 0.98\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("Tension", math=r"T = 2(9.8 - 0.98) = 17.64\ \text{N}"),
        ], check=r"17.64 - 14.7 = 2.94 = 3(0.98)"),
        MISTAKE("Friction in the wrong direction", r"Friction opposes the motion of the particle **relative to the surface**. A on the table moves towards the pulley, so friction acts away from the pulley."),
        MISTAKE("Taking $R = mg$ automatically", r"Resolve vertically every time. If there is a vertical component of an applied force, $R$ is not $mg$."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A wooden box of mass 12 kg rests on a rough horizontal floor with $\mu = 0.35$. A horizontal force of 35 N is applied. Show that the box does not move and state the friction force.",
           r"$R = 12(9.8) = 117.6$ N, so $F_{\max} = 0.35 \times 117.6 = 41.16$ N. The push (35 N) is less than 41.16 N, so friction can balance it and the box stays at rest. The friction force is $35$ N (not 41.2 N)."),
        PQ(r"A crate of mass 40 kg lies on a rough horizontal floor with $\mu = 0.45$. Find the smallest horizontal force that will make it move.",
           r"$R = 40(9.8) = 392$ N. The crate is on the point of moving when $P = \mu R = 0.45 \times 392 = 176.4$ N. So $P$ must exceed about 176 N."),
        PQ(r"Particle A of mass 5 kg lies on a rough horizontal table ($\mu = 0.3$), joined by a light string over a smooth pulley at the edge of the table to particle B of mass 3 kg, which hangs. The system is released from rest. Find the acceleration and the tension. After B has fallen 1.2 m it hits the floor. How much further does A slide before stopping?",
           r"A: $T - 0.3(5)(9.8) = 5a$, so $T - 14.7 = 5a$. B: $29.4 - T = 3a$. Add: $14.7 = 8a$, $a = 1.84\ \text{m/s}^2$. $T = 3(9.8 - 1.8375) = 23.9$ N. At the floor: $v^2 = 2(1.8375)(1.2) = 4.41$. The string goes slack; A decelerates at $\mu g = 2.94\ \text{m/s}^2$. $0 = 4.41 - 2(2.94)s$, so $s = 0.75$ m."),
        PQ(r"A crate of mass 25 kg is pushed along a rough horizontal floor ($\mu = 0.3$) by a force of 100 N directed $20^\circ$ **below** the horizontal. Find the acceleration.",
           r"$R = 25(9.8) + 100\sin 20^\circ = 245 + 34.2 = 279.2$ N. $F = 0.3 \times 279.2 = 83.8$ N. $100\cos 20^\circ - 83.8 = 25a$, so $a = (93.97 - 83.76)/25 = 0.408\ \text{m/s}^2$."),
        TIP(r"In the exam: friction appears in Paper 3 (mechanics, essay questions) and in the mechanics section of Paper 1 (multiple choice). State clearly 'friction is limiting, so $F = \mu R$' or 'the particle is at rest, so $F$ is found from equilibrium'. Examiners look for that sentence."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Friction opposes (the tendency of) sliding and satisfies $F \le \mu R$.",
            r"At rest and not about to move: find $F$ from equilibrium; $F < \mu R$. About to slip or sliding: $F = \mu R$.",
            r"Find $R$ first by resolving perpendicular to the surface. A pull upwards at an angle reduces $R$; a push downwards increases it.",
            r"Check that the particle moves: the applied force along the surface must exceed $\mu R$.",
            r"When sliding, $F_{\text{resultant}} = ma$ along the surface, with friction opposing the motion."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. Motion on an inclined plane
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MODULE,
    topic="Motion on an inclined plane",
    title="Particles on smooth and rough slopes",
    slug="mel2-inclined-plane-motion",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Resolve along and perpendicular to a slope to find the acceleration of a particle on a smooth or rough plane, decide whether it slides, and solve problems with an applied force or a pulley.",
    objectives=[
        "Resolve the weight into $mg\\sin\\theta$ along the slope and $mg\\cos\\theta$ perpendicular to it",
        "Find the acceleration of a particle sliding down or pulled up a smooth or rough slope",
        "Decide whether a particle on a rough slope stays at rest, using $\\tan\\theta$ and $\\mu$",
        "Solve connected-particle problems with a slope and a pulley, including the force on the pulley",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Component of weight", "Normal reaction", "Friction on a slope", "Angle of friction", "Pulley on a slope"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A bus on the steep road down from Bamenda to the plain near Bafut stays on the road because of friction. A crate slides down the ramp of a lorry in Douala port. Both are particles on an **inclined plane** (a slope). You learned in Form 5 to resolve forces on a smooth slope. Here we add friction and connected particles, and we use $g = 9.8\ \text{m/s}^2$."),
        H("Resolving on a slope"),
        T(r"On a slope inclined at $\theta$ to the horizontal, the two natural directions are **along** the slope and **perpendicular** to it. The weight $mg$ acts vertically downwards, so it has two components:"),
        IMG("apomel2-incline-rough.svg", alt="A block on a slope at 30 degrees with weight mg downwards, normal reaction R perpendicular to the slope, friction F up the slope, and the components mg sin theta along the slope and mg cos theta into the slope", caption="Figure 1. Forces on a block sliding down a rough slope inclined at $\\theta$."),
        D(r"Along the slope (down), the component of the weight is $mg\sin\theta$. Perpendicular to the slope (into it), the component is $mg\cos\theta$. There is no motion perpendicular to the slope, so the normal reaction is", title="Resolving the weight", formula=r"R = mg\cos\theta \quad\text{(if no other force has a component perpendicular to the slope)}", note=r"Check: at $\theta = 0$ the slope is flat, $R = mg$ and the along-slope component is $0$. At $\theta = 90^\circ$ the slope is a wall: $R = 0$ and the component is $mg$."),
        T(r"For a smooth slope there is no friction. Down the slope: $mg\sin\theta = ma$, so"),
        F(r"a = g\sin\theta"),
        T(r"The mass cancels. A 1 kg stone and a 100 kg crate slide down the same smooth slope with the same acceleration. For $\theta = 30^\circ$, $a = 9.8 \times 0.5 = 4.9\ \text{m/s}^2$."),
        H("A rough slope"),
        EX("Worked example 1: sliding down a rough slope", intro=r"A block of mass 6 kg is released from rest on a rough slope inclined at $30^\circ$ to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction is $0.2$. Show that the block slides down, and find its acceleration and its speed after 2 s.", steps=[
            S("Perpendicular to the slope", math=r"R = 6(9.8)\cos 30^\circ = 50.92\ \text{N}"),
            S("Limiting friction", math=r"F_{\max} = 0.2 \times 50.92 = 10.18\ \text{N}"),
            S("Component of the weight down the slope", math=r"6(9.8)\sin 30^\circ = 29.4\ \text{N} > 10.18\ \text{N}", text="So the block slides, and friction acts **up** the slope."),
            S("Newton's second law down the slope", math=r"6(9.8)\sin 30^\circ - 0.2(6)(9.8)\cos 30^\circ = 6a"),
            S("Divide by the mass (it cancels)", math=r"a = 9.8(\sin 30^\circ - 0.2\cos 30^\circ) = 9.8(0.5 - 0.1732) = 3.20\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("Speed after 2 s from rest", math=r"v = 0 + 3.20 \times 2 = 6.41\ \text{m/s}"),
        ], check=r"29.4 - 10.18 = 19.22 = 6(3.203)"),
        KEY("General result for a block sliding down a rough slope", "", cards=[
            ["Acceleration", r"$a = g(\sin\theta - \mu\cos\theta)$, if this is positive."],
            ["It slides if", r"$\tan\theta > \mu$. That is, $mg\sin\theta > \mu mg\cos\theta$."],
            ["Angle of friction", r"The angle $\lambda$ with $\tan\lambda = \mu$: a block rests on a slope if $\theta \le \lambda$ and slides if $\theta > \lambda$."],
        ]),
        QUIZ(r"A block rests on a rough slope inclined at $\theta$ with $\tan\theta = 0.4$. For which value of $\mu$ does the block stay at rest?",
             [r"$\mu = 0.3$", r"$\mu = 0.4$ or more", r"$\mu = 0.2$", r"$\mu = 0$"], 1,
             r"It rests if $\mu \ge \tan\theta = 0.4$: then the friction needed, $mg\sin\theta$, does not exceed the limiting friction $\mu mg\cos\theta$.",
             hint=r"Compare $\mu$ with $\tan\theta$.",
             feedback=[r"With $\mu = 0.3 < 0.4$ the limiting friction is too small to hold the block: it slides.", "", r"With $\mu = 0.2 < 0.4$ the block slides.", "A smooth slope cannot hold a block at rest."]),
        MISTAKE("Using $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\cos\\theta$ the wrong way round", r"Along the slope is $mg\sin\theta$ and perpendicular to the slope is $mg\cos\theta$ when $\theta$ is the angle of the slope. Test with $\theta = 0$: a flat surface has no along-slope component."),
        MISTAKE("Friction in the wrong direction", r"Friction opposes the **motion** (or the tendency to move) along the slope. For a block sliding down, friction acts up the slope. For a block pushed up, friction acts down the slope."),
        H("A block at rest on a rough slope, and a push up the slope"),
        IMG("apomel2-incline-block-q.svg", alt="A block of mass 10 kg on a rough slope inclined at 25 degrees to the horizontal", caption="Figure 2. A 10 kg block on a rough slope inclined at $25^{\\circ}$ (Worked example 2)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: at rest, and the force to move it up", intro=r"A block of mass 10 kg is on a rough slope inclined at $25^\circ$ to the horizontal, with $\mu = 0.5$ (Figure 2). (a) Show that the block stays at rest and find the friction force. (b) Find the least force $P$ applied up the slope, parallel to it, that will make the block move up.", steps=[
            S("(a) $\\tan 25^\\circ = 0.466$, which is less than $\\mu = 0.5$", text="So the block does **not** slide."),
            S("The friction needed balances the component of the weight", math=r"F = 10(9.8)\sin 25^\circ = 41.4\ \text{N}"),
            S("Check against the limit", math=r"\mu R = 0.5 \times 10(9.8)\cos 25^\circ = 44.4\ \text{N} > 41.4\ \text{N}", text="The friction force is **41.4 N** up the slope, not 44.4 N, because the block is not about to slip."),
            S("(b) On the point of moving up, friction is limiting and acts **down** the slope", math=r"P = mg\sin 25^\circ + \mu mg\cos 25^\circ = 41.4 + 44.4"),
            S("Solve", math=r"P = 85.8\ \text{N}"),
        ], check=r"85.8 - 41.4 - 44.4 = 0"),
        EX("Worked example 3: acceleration up a rough slope", intro=r"A block of mass 8 kg is pulled up a rough slope inclined at $20^\circ$ by a force of 60 N acting up the slope and parallel to it. The coefficient of friction is $0.3$. Find the acceleration.", steps=[
            S("Perpendicular to the slope", math=r"R = 8(9.8)\cos 20^\circ = 73.67\ \text{N}"),
            S("Friction while sliding up (acts down the slope)", math=r"F = 0.3 \times 73.67 = 22.10\ \text{N}"),
            S("Component of the weight down the slope", math=r"8(9.8)\sin 20^\circ = 26.81\ \text{N}"),
            S("Newton's second law up the slope", math=r"60 - 26.81 - 22.10 = 8a"),
            S("Solve", math=r"a = \frac{11.09}{8} = 1.39\ \text{m/s}^2"),
        ], check=r"26.81 + 22.10 + 8(1.386) = 60"),
        H("A slope and a pulley"),
        IMG("apomel2-incline-pulley.svg", alt="A block A of mass 4 kg on a rough slope at 30 degrees joined by a string over a smooth pulley at the top of the slope to a hanging block B of mass 3 kg", caption="Figure 3. A on a rough slope, B hanging. The string between A and the pulley is parallel to the slope."),
        EX("Worked example 4: pulley, slope and force on the pulley", intro=r"Particle A of mass 4 kg is on a rough plane inclined at $30^\circ$ ($\mu = 0.25$). It is joined by a light string, passing over a smooth pulley at the top of the plane, to particle B of mass 3 kg hanging freely. The system is released from rest. Find (a) the acceleration, (b) the tension, (c) the magnitude of the force of the string on the pulley.", steps=[
            S("Is it going to move up? Compare B's weight with A's tendency to slide down plus the limiting friction", math=r"3(9.8) = 29.4\ \text{N} > 4(9.8)\sin 30^\circ + 0.25 \times 4(9.8)\cos 30^\circ = 19.6 + 8.49 = 28.09\ \text{N}", text="B is heavier by a little, so A moves **up** the slope and friction acts **down** it."),
            S("A, up the slope", math=r"T - 19.6 - 8.49 = 4a"),
            S("B, downwards", math=r"29.4 - T = 3a"),
            S("Add", math=r"29.4 - 28.09 = 7a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 0.188\ \text{m/s}^2"),
            S("Tension, from B", math=r"T = 3(9.8 - 0.1876) = 28.8\ \text{N}"),
            S("(c) The string parts leave the pulley along the slope (down at $30^\\circ$ below the horizontal) and vertically down. The angle between them is $60^\\circ$", math=r"F = 2T\cos 30^\circ = 2(28.84)(0.866) = 49.9\ \text{N}"),
        ], check=r"28.84 - 28.09 = 0.75 = 4(0.188)"),
        TIP(r"The Board's report on a connected-particle question says candidates did not use the angle at the pulley to find the force on it. Draw both string directions from the pulley, find the angle between them, then use $2T\cos(\varphi/2)$."),
        PQ(r"A particle slides down a smooth plane inclined at $40^\circ$ to the horizontal, starting from rest. Find its acceleration and the distance it travels in 3 s.",
           r"$a = g\sin 40^\circ = 9.8(0.6428) = 6.30\ \text{m/s}^2$. $s = \tfrac12(6.30)(3^2) = 28.3$ m."),
        PQ(r"A crate of mass 20 kg is released from rest on a rough slope inclined at $35^\circ$ to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction is $0.4$. Find the acceleration, and the speed after it has slid 5 m.",
           r"$a = 9.8(\sin 35^\circ - 0.4\cos 35^\circ) = 9.8(0.5736 - 0.3277) = 2.41\ \text{m/s}^2$. $v^2 = 2(2.41)(5) = 24.1$, so $v = 4.91\ \text{m/s}$."),
        PQ(r"A block of mass 5 kg is held at rest on a rough slope inclined at $30^\circ$ by a force $P$ acting up the slope and parallel to it. The coefficient of friction is $0.2$. Find the range of values of $P$ for which the block stays at rest.",
           r"$mg\sin 30^\circ = 24.5$ N and limiting friction $= 0.2(5)(9.8)\cos 30^\circ = 8.49$ N. About to slide down: $P = 24.5 - 8.49 = 16.0$ N. About to slide up: $P = 24.5 + 8.49 = 33.0$ N. So $16.0 \le P \le 33.0$ N."),
        PQ(r"Particle A of mass 5 kg is on a rough plane inclined at $30^\circ$ with $\mu = 0.2$. It is joined by a light string over a smooth pulley at the top of the plane to a hanging particle B of mass 2 kg. The system is released from rest. Show that it stays at rest and find the friction force.",
           r"The weight component down the slope on A is $5(9.8)\sin 30^\circ = 24.5$ N, and B pulls A up the slope with at most its weight, $2(9.8) = 19.6$ N. So A has a net tendency to slide down, of $24.5 - 19.6 = 4.9$ N. Limiting friction $= 0.2 \times 5(9.8)\cos 30^\circ = 8.49$ N, which is greater than 4.9 N, so the system stays at rest, with friction $4.9$ N acting up the slope."),
        TIP(r"In the exam: motion on a slope, with or without friction, appears in Paper 3 (mechanics) and in the mechanics section of Paper 1 (multiple choice). Draw the slope with the angle marked, resolve along and perpendicular to it, and say whether friction is limiting."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"On a slope at angle $\theta$: along the slope $mg\sin\theta$, perpendicular $mg\cos\theta$, so $R = mg\cos\theta$ (with no other perpendicular force).",
            r"Smooth slope: $a = g\sin\theta$. Rough slope, sliding down: $a = g(\sin\theta - \mu\cos\theta)$.",
            r"The block stays at rest if $\tan\theta \le \mu$; then friction is $mg\sin\theta$, which is less than $\mu R$ unless limiting.",
            r"Friction acts down the slope if the block moves or tends to move up; up the slope otherwise.",
            r"With a pulley, write $F = ma$ for each particle; the force on the pulley is $2T\cos(\varphi/2)$ with $\varphi$ the angle between the string parts."),
    ],
))

